# EP01 — What Happens to Your Body If You Stay Awake for 11 Days
# Real case: Randy Gardner, San Diego, 28 Dec 1963 – 8 Jan 1964 (264 hours).
# Narration tags like [S3] point to the sources at the bottom; they are stripped before the voice is made.
METER: TIME AWAKE
CASE: CASE 01 / R. GARDNER, 1964
SPRITE you | {you} standing, front view, arms relaxed, calm curious expression
SPRITE tired | {you} standing and swaying, exhausted droopy half-closed eyes, dark rings under the eyes, shoulders slumped
SPRITE brain | a human brain glowing cyan, side view, a few spots glowing orange
SPRITE coke | a single aluminium soda can standing upright, glowing cyan outline with an orange stripe, no person
SPRITE ball | a basketball glowing orange line art
SPRITE rat | a small cartoon lab rat glowing cyan, tired eyes
SPRITE fly | a single tiny cartoon fruit fly insect with wings and six legs, glowing cyan with big orange eyes, no person
SPRITE pillow | a single soft pillow glowing cyan
# id | picture | tokens | caption | narration
# ---- HOOK ----
h01 | {you} sitting on the edge of a bed at night with huge wide-open bloodshot eyes, a big round clock on the wall, a window with a moon | zoomin glow hr=96 temp=36.4 t=DAY_11 s=CRIT o2=97 | DAY 11. NO SLEEP. | It's day eleven. You haven't slept for two hundred and sixty-four hours. [S1]
h02 | close up of {you} face with enormous tired eyes, one eye twitching, sweat drops, cyan glow | hold | | Your eyes won't focus. Your words come out slurred. Somewhere in your brain, little patches are switching off by themselves, a few seconds at a time, and you don't even notice. [S1, S9]
h03 | {you} standing in a doorway and pointing at a hallway, but the hallway wall is full of ghostly glowing faces and shapes, spooky | glow panr | | You're seeing things that aren't there. And you're pretty sure the people around you are up to something. [S1]
h04 | =h01 | zoomout | | And here's the strange part. Back then, scientists honestly didn't know if this would kill you. In just over a week, a seventeen year old was going to find out, live, in front of the national press. [S2]
h05 | - | spr:you@540,1010,0.95,on,breathe hr=72 temp=36.8 t=HOUR_0 s=OK o2=98 dust | HOUR 0 | So let's do it properly. You. No sleep. Eleven days. Hour by hour, organ by organ, with a vitals monitor that will slowly stop being funny. Let's go.
# ---- HOUR 0: THE BET ----
s01 | two cartoon teenagers glowing cyan flipping a glowing coin in the air between them, a school science fair poster board behind them with no text | pop panl | San Diego, 1963 | San Diego, December nineteen sixty-three. Two high school boys need a science fair project. Their idea? Stay awake as long as humanly possible. [S2]
s02 | =s01 | zoomin | | They flip a coin to decide who has to do it. One of them wins. The other one is Randy Gardner. Today, you are Randy. [S2]
s03 | a glowing radio microphone and a vinyl record, a tiny palm tree beside them, cyan and orange line art | sparks | Record to beat: 260 h | The record to beat belongs to a radio DJ in Honolulu. Two hundred and sixty hours. Just under eleven days. Easy. You've stayed up late before. [S2]
s04 | - | spr:coke@380,900,0.35,drop,float spr:you@640,1010,0.95,on,breathe | No pills. Just Coke. | The rules are simple. No pills, no stimulants. Your helpers later say you had the occasional Coke, and that's it. [S2]
# ---- HOUR 17: LEGALLY DRUNK ----
s05 | {you} sitting at a kitchen table late at night yawning hugely, an empty coffee mug, a dim lamp | zoomin hr=78 t=HOUR_17 s=OK | | The first night feels like a sleepover. Then the sun comes up, and your body starts sending invoices.
s06 | {you} trying to walk in a straight line on a glowing floor line but wobbling, a glowing cocktail glass floating next to the person | glow hold hr=80 s=WARN | 17 hours = 0.05% alcohol | At around seventeen to nineteen hours awake, researchers found that people performed on some tests as badly as someone with a blood alcohol level of zero point zero five percent. [S3]
s07 | =s06 | zoomout | Up to 50% slower | Reaction times on some tests were up to fifty percent slower. You haven't had a single drink. Your brain just behaves like it's had a couple. [S3]
s08 | a glowing car steering wheel seen from the driver seat at night, empty road ahead, cyan line art, a small orange warning light | panr | | And keep going a few more hours, and it got worse, as bad as the highest alcohol dose they tested. That's why you're not allowed anywhere near a car in this video. [S3]
# ---- HOUR 24: THE FIRST CRACKS ----
s09 | {you} with a giant glowing brain inside the head, a small orange glow deep in the middle of the brain, the person looks grumpy | glow zoomin hr=84 t=HOUR_24 | | Twenty-four hours. A whole day. And deep in your brain, a small almond-shaped alarm system called the amygdala is getting louder.
s10 | =s09 | glow hold | Amygdala: ~60% more reactive | In one brain scan study, people who'd been kept awake for about a day and a half reacted to upsetting pictures with around sixty percent more amygdala activity than people who slept. [S6]
s11 | {you} angrily pointing at a tiny innocent glowing toaster on a kitchen counter, the person looks furious with steam above the head | pop panl | | Meanwhile, the part of your brain that normally says, calm down, it's just a toaster, has gone quiet. So everything feels personal. Even the toaster. [S6]
s12 | {you} opening a glowing fridge at night with hungry eyes, the fridge light spilling out, junk food inside | zoomin | | And you're hungry. Weirdly hungry. Short sleep is linked to lower levels of leptin, the hormone that says you're full, and higher ghrelin, the hormone that says, feed me. [S7]
s13 | =s12 | hold | | That's why at three in the morning a salad has never once called your name, but cold pizza has.
# ---- DAY 2: MICROSLEEPS ----
s14 | {you} sitting at a desk with head dropping forward, eyes closed for a moment, a small glowing clock, papers on the desk | zoomin hr=82 temp=36.6 t=DAY_2 s=WARN | MICROSLEEP | Day two. Now the strangest thing starts happening. Your brain begins to fall asleep without asking you. [S9]
s15 | split view drawing: a glowing brain with half of it dark and sleeping and half bright and awake, cyan and dark blue | glow hold | A few seconds. No warning. | These are called microsleeps. Tiny episodes of sleep that last a few seconds, sometimes up to fifteen. From the inside, it feels like nothing happened. The world just skips forward. [S9]
s16 | =s08 | panl | | At a desk, that's embarrassing. On a road, it's how a car travels the length of a football field with nobody really driving it. [S9]
s17 | {you} playing basketball, jumping toward a glowing hoop, dynamic pose, bright orange ball | pop panr | | So your friends keep you moving. Basketball. Bowling. Anything. Because the moment you close your eyes, you are asleep. Instantly. [S2]
s18 | =s17 | hold | | Funny detail. Your basketball actually gets better. Partly because you're fit, and partly because you're playing basketball every night instead of sleeping. [S2]
# ---- DAY 3: YOUR SENSES GO WEIRD ----
s19 | {you} holding the nose with a disgusted face, wavy glowing smell lines coming from a small sandwich | glow zoomin hr=86 t=DAY_3 | | Day three. Your senses start to glitch. The scientists test your taste, your smell, your hearing, and things are getting strange. [S2]
s20 | =s19 | hold | "Don't make me smell that." | You keep begging them, don't make me smell that, I can't stand the smell. Ordinary smells now feel like an attack. [S2]
s21 | a glowing human brain with tiny glowing cleaning robots and little rivers of blue fluid washing through it, cute | glow panl | | And here's what your brain is quietly missing. In studies on mice, sleep opened up the spaces between brain cells by about sixty percent, letting fluid wash through and carry waste away. [S8]
s22 | =s21 | zoomin | Waste piling up | Awake, that cleaning crew works much more slowly. One of the things it normally clears is beta amyloid, a protein linked to Alzheimer's disease. You are now three days behind on the bins. [S8]
# ---- DAY 4: THE MIND CRACKS ----
s23 | {you} looking at a lamp post that looks a bit like a person, the shadow of the lamp post is a spooky person shape, dark street | glow zoomin hr=90 temp=36.5 t=DAY_4 s=WARN | | By day four, things get properly weird.
s24 | =h03 | glow hold | Hallucinations. Paranoia. | Lieutenant Commander John Ross, the navy doctor checking your health, later reported moodiness, trouble concentrating, short term memory problems, paranoia, and hallucinations. [S1]
s25 | a glowing professor with glasses and a lab coat holding a clipboard, standing next to a pinball machine with flashing lights | pop panr | | But the famous Stanford sleep scientist who joins the experiment, William Dement, sees something else. On day ten, you beat him at pinball. [S1]
s26 | =s25 | hold | | So which is it? Are you falling apart, or are you fine? The honest answer is, both. And the reason why is the weirdest part of this whole story.
s27 | =s15 | glow zoomin | | Your brain seems to be catnapping in pieces. Some parts asleep, other parts awake, taking turns. One of your friends later says your brain had been catnapping the entire time. [S2]
s28 | - | spr:tired@540,1010,0.95,on,sway dust | | It's not a superpower. It's damage control. You're not really awake anymore. You're just not completely asleep.
# ---- DAY 6: YOUR BODY FIGHTS BACK ----
s29 | {you} standing with a glowing shield that has holes in it, tiny glowing germs flying toward the person, the person looks worried | glow panl hr=92 temp=36.4 t=DAY_6 | | Day six. Your immune system is running on fumes too. Sleep is when a lot of immune maintenance happens.
s30 | =s29 | zoomin | | In studies, people who skipped sleep the night after a vaccine often made a weaker immune response to it. Your body is too busy surviving tonight to plan for next month. [S13]
s31 | {you} holding up a glowing calendar page and staring at it confused, question marks floating around, a calendar with blank squares | pop hold | | And your memory? You're forgetting what you said a minute ago. You're losing the thread halfway through sentences. [S1]
s32 | a newspaper printing press with glowing newspapers flying out, cyan and orange line art, no readable text | panr | 3rd biggest story in the US | Meanwhile, outside, you've become famous. For a short time, your experiment is the third most written about story in the American press, behind the Kennedy assassination and the Beatles. [S2]
# ---- WHAT KILLS YOU WITHOUT SLEEP? ----
s33 | - | spr:rat@540,800,0.5,pop,bob dust hr=92 t=DAY_8 | | So, day eight. Can this actually kill you? Here's where it gets dark.
s34 | a small cartoon lab rat glowing cyan standing on a round disc above shallow water inside a glass lab box, sad eyes | glow zoomin | Rats: 11 to 32 days | In the nineteen eighties, researchers kept rats awake on a turning disc above water. Every single rat died, or was put down because death looked close, within eleven to thirty-two days. [S10]
s35 | =s34 | hold | Ate more. Lost weight. | They ate more food than ever and still lost weight. Their bodies were burning energy at more than twice the normal rate. And the scientists couldn't find a clear cause of death. [S10]
s36 | - | spr:fly@540,700,0.45,pop,float dust | | It took until twenty twenty to find a big clue. In fruit flies and mice, sleep loss caused a build up of harmful molecules called reactive oxygen species. Not in the brain. In the gut. [S11]
s37 | a glowing cartoon fruit fly drinking from a tiny glowing medicine bottle, happy, little sparkles | pop hold | Antioxidants = survived | And when the scientists neutralized those molecules with antioxidants, the flies lived a normal lifespan with little to no sleep. Please do not try this with smoothies. [S11]
s38 | =h01 | glow zoomout | | In humans, there's a rare genetic disease called fatal familial insomnia, where the brain gradually loses the ability to sleep. People with it die, on average, around eighteen months after symptoms start. There is no known cure. [S12]
s39 | =s38 | hold | | Which is why, after people kept trying to beat your record, Guinness World Records stopped certifying sleep deprivation attempts. Too dangerous. [S2]
# ---- DAY 11: THE FINISH LINE ----
s40 | {you} slumped against a wall with very heavy eyelids, holding a phone to the ear, glowing and dim, exhausted | glow zoomin hr=96 temp=36.4 t=DAY_11 s=CRIT | | Day eleven. Your attention keeps slipping. One scientist later describes you as awake, but basically not working. [S1, S14]
s41 | =s03 | sparks | 264 h 25 min | And then, it's done. Eleven days and twenty-five minutes. Two hundred and sixty-four hours. The DJ's record is broken. [S1]
s42 | a crowd of glowing cartoon reporters with cameras and microphones in front of a small house, camera flashes | pop panr | "Mind over matter." | There's a press conference outside your parents' house. Someone asks how you did it. You say, it's just mind over matter. [S2]
# ---- THE CRASH ----
s43 | {you} sleeping deeply in a hospital bed with many glowing wires on the head connected to a brain wave monitor, peaceful smile | zzz zoomin hr=58 temp=36.6 o2=98 t=SLEEP s=OK | Slept: 14 h 40 min | Then they take you to a naval hospital, wire up your head, and let you sleep. You sleep for about fourteen hours and forty minutes. And then you wake up, because you need the toilet. [S1, S2]
s44 | =s15 | glow hold | REM rebound | On that first night, the share of dream sleep, REM, shoots up. Your brain is paying back its debt, with interest. The next nights it settles back down to normal. [S2]
s45 | {you} walking to school with a backpack, sun in the sky, cheerful, a small glowing school building | walkl panr hr=72 t=DAY_13 | | And then, unbelievably, you get up and go back to high school.
# ---- THE BILL ARRIVES ----
s46 | =h01 | glow zoomin hr=74 t=YEARS_LATER s=WARN | | You seem fine. But decades later, Randy says the price came later. Years of awful insomnia. The boy who stayed awake for eleven days ended up struggling to sleep at all. [S2]
s47 | {you} lying in bed at night looking at a glowing phone with a tired face, the clock showing late night, a glowing cup of coffee on the nightstand | panl hr=76 t=YOUR_WEEK s=WARN | | Now look at your own week. Six hours a night feels normal for a lot of people. But in lab studies, sleeping six hours a night for two weeks built up deficits in attention and memory similar to one to three nights of no sleep at all. [S4, S5]
s48 | =s47 | hold | You don't feel it. You just are it. | And the worst part? People on six hours stopped feeling much sleepier after the first few days. The tests kept getting worse. They just stopped noticing. [S4]
# ---- OUTRO ----
s49 | {you} sleeping happily curled up in a cozy bed with a glowing pillow, a smiling moon in the window | zzz zoomin hr=60 temp=36.7 o2=98 t=8_HOURS s=OK | | So here's your prescription from this channel. Sleep tonight. It's the cheapest upgrade your body will ever get.
s50 | - | spr:you@540,1010,0.95,on,breathe dust | | And if you're watching this at three in the morning, I mean, I can't judge you. You're the one who clicked a sleep video instead of sleeping.
s51 | - | endcard spr:you@330,980,0.8,none,breathe hr=72 t=NEXT s=OK | NEXT: THE MARIANA TRENCH | Next week, you fall into the deepest place on Earth, and we follow your body all the way down. Eleven kilometres of water, and one very bad swimming day. See you at the bottom.
# ---- SOURCES ----
# [S1] Wikipedia, "Randy Gardner sleep deprivation experiment": 11 days 25 min (264.4 h), 1963–64; William Dement attended; Lt Cmdr John J. Ross monitored health and reported moodiness, problems with concentration and short-term memory, paranoia and hallucinations; Dement: beat him at pinball on day 10; slept 14 h 46 min afterwards. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Randy_Gardner_sleep_deprivation_experiment
# [S2] Sarah Keating, "The boy who stayed awake for 11 days", BBC Future, 18 Jan 2018: science fair project, coin toss, previous record 260 h by a Honolulu DJ, occasional Cokes and no stimulants, smell sensitivity, basketball improved, "if he closed his eyes he would be immediately asleep", third most written-about story after JFK's assassination and the Beatles, naval hospital, slept ~14 h, REM rebound, "catnapping", Guinness stopped certifying attempts, later years of insomnia, "mind over matter". https://www.bbc.com/future/article/20180118-the-boy-who-stayed-awake-for-11-days
# [S3] Williamson AM, Feyer AM. Moderate sleep deprivation produces impairments in cognitive and motor performance equivalent to legally prescribed levels of alcohol intoxication. Occup Environ Med 2000;57:649–655 (17–19 h awake ≈ BAC 0.05% on some tests; responses up to 50% slower; longer ≈ BAC 0.1%). https://doi.org/10.1136/oem.57.10.649
# [S4] Van Dongen HP, Maislin G, Mullington JM, Dinges DF. The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness. Sleep 2003;26(2):117–126 (6 h/night for 14 days → cumulative deficits; sleepiness ratings rose only slightly). https://doi.org/10.1093/sleep/26.2.117
# [S5] Banks S, Dinges DF. Behavioral and physiological consequences of sleep restriction. J Clin Sleep Med 2007;3(5):519–528 (deficits equivalent to 1–3 nights of total sleep loss). https://doi.org/10.5664/jcsm.26918
# [S6] Yoo SS, Gujar N, Hu P, Jolesz FA, Walker MP. The human emotional brain without sleep — a prefrontal amygdala disconnect. Curr Biol 2007;17(20):R877–878 (≈35 h awake: ~60% greater amygdala reactivity to negative pictures; weaker prefrontal connectivity). https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cub.2007.08.007
# [S7] Taheri S, Lin L, Austin D, Young T, Mignot E. Short sleep duration is associated with reduced leptin, elevated ghrelin, and increased body mass index. PLoS Med 2004;1(3):e62. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pmed.0010062
# [S8] Xie L, Kang H, Xu Q, et al. Sleep drives metabolite clearance from the adult brain. Science 2013;342:373–377 (mice: 60% increase in interstitial space during sleep; faster β-amyloid clearance). https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1241224
# [S9] Wikipedia, "Microsleep" and "Sleep deprivation": episodes of a few seconds, usually no longer than 15 s; risk while driving. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsleep
# [S10] Everson CA, Bergmann BM, Rechtschaffen A. Sleep deprivation in the rat: III. Total sleep deprivation. Sleep 1989;12(1):13–21 (all died or were sacrificed within 11–32 days; ate more yet lost weight; energy expenditure > 2× baseline; no anatomical cause found). https://doi.org/10.1093/sleep/12.1.13
# [S11] Vaccaro A, Kaplan Dor Y, Nambara K, et al. Sleep loss can cause death through accumulation of reactive oxygen species in the gut. Cell 2020;181(6):1307–1328. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2020.04.049
# [S12] Wikipedia, "Fatal familial insomnia" (average survival ~18 months from onset; no disease-modifying treatment). https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fatal_familial_insomnia
# [S13] Wikipedia, "Sleep deprivation", section on the immune system (poorer vaccine response after a sleepless night). https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sleep_deprivation
# [S14] Scientific American, "How long can humans stay awake?" (Gardner "basically cognitively dysfunctional" at the end). https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/how-long-can-humans-stay/
# ---- FACT-CHECK ----
# - Dates: BBC says it ended 8 Jan 1964; start therefore 28 Dec 1963. Some sources (SciAm) say 1965 — we use 1963–64 (Wikipedia, BBC).
# - Recovery sleep: Wikipedia 14 h 46 min, BBC "14 hours". Narration says "about fourteen hours and forty minutes" — OK.
# - "Ten years earlier scientists didn't know if it would kill you": BBC quotes Dement that the question "was still unresolved" in 1964 — narration says "honestly didn't know", OK.
# - Amygdala 60%: from Yoo 2007 (~35 h). Narration says "about a day and a half" — OK.
# - Hallucinations/paranoia attributed to Ross (via Wikipedia). We don't date them to a specific day.
# - Six-hour figure: Van Dongen (14 days of 6 h) and Banks & Dinges (1–3 nights). Narration says "one to three nights" — OK.
# - Rats/flies are animal studies; narration says so. Antioxidant line ends with "do not try this" — no health advice implied.
# - Vaccine response: general statement, sourced to the Wikipedia section (which cites the primary studies). Consider re-checking the primary paper before upload.
